## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a CPU-friendly, real-time anomaly detector under label scarcity; covers preprocessing, model pick, online updates, drift, calibration, and evaluation in a streaming context.

## Key Concepts

- Online/streaming learning
- CPU-friendly models and latency budgeting
- Concept drift detection and calibration
- Imbalanced anomaly evaluation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for partial_fit usage (sketched)
const lr = new LogisticRegression({penalty:'l2', C:1e-4});
while (streaming){
  const batch = getNextBatch(1000);
  lr.partial_fit(batch.features, batch.labels, [0,1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would label delay or feedback latency affect retraining cadence?
- How would you monitor for false positives increasing over time and adapt thresholds?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical lightweight baseline building for text routing under strict latency constraints, in a data-scarce regime with model calibration requirements. Also covers drift monitoring and retraining cadence.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight text representation with a fixed 30-feature vector
- SGD-based multiclass logistic regression or tiny decision tree for efficiency
- Calibration via temperature scaling
- Drift detection using rolling holdout validation
- Deployment latency and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Training loop outline for SGD multiclass LR
const model = new SGDLogisticRegression({
  learningRate: 0.01,
  epochs: 100,
  batchSize: 32
});
model.fit(XTrain, yTrain);
// Temperature scaling for calibration
const temp = calibrateTemperature(model, XVal, yVal);
// Inference
const predict = (features) => {
  const start = performance.now();
  const probs = model.predictProb(features, temp);
  const latency = performance.now() - start;
  return { probs, latency };
};
```
